WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.257
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.397 --> 00:00:08.230
SPEAKER: Welcome to the
quick start tutorial

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.160
for the BigQuery web UI.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.200
Head over to the
navigation menu and find

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.120
BigQuery listed under Big Data.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:21.545
BigQuery provides some public
data sets that we can access.

00:00:21.545 --> 00:00:23.170
For the first part
of this quick start,

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.080
we'll use a data set of
USA names between the years

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.870
1910 and 2013.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.030
Here is a query editor.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.480
Let's find out what the most
common names are in our data

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.760
set.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.390
On the lower right side,
there's a green check mark icon.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.385
When we click it, it
runs a query validator

00:00:41.385 --> 00:00:43.140
that will tell us
if our query is

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.980
valid without having to run it.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.470
If our query is invalid, it
will tell us the error so

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.290
that we can go back
and correct it.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.590
Otherwise, we're good to go.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.930
Click Run to run our query.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.420
The results will appear below.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.970
We can view the query results
as a table or in JSON format.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.830
We also have the option
to save the results.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.990
How about if we want
to load our own data?

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.000
To check this out,
we'll use a text file

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.830
that contains the most common
US baby names of the year 2014.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.450
This is what the
file looks like--

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.130
a Comma-Separated Value file,
CSV, with three columns.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.140
To load this test
data into BigQuery,

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.120
let's create a data set.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.000
In the navigation panel,
in the Resources section,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.650
click your project name.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.530
Then, over on the right,
in the details panel,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.440
click Create Dataset.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.350
For data set ID,
let's enter babynames.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.930
For data location,
we'll put United States.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.690
We can leave all other fields
with their default values,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.390
and click Create Dataset.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:01:59.590
We've got a data set.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.350
Now we can load the
data into a new table.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.480
Back over in the
Resources section,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.840
we'll see our babynames
data set under our project.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.630
Click it.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.850
In the details panel,
click Create Table.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.750
Let's change the source
from Empty Table to Upload.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.120
Click Browse, and find the
CSV file with our data in it.

00:02:24.198 --> 00:02:26.490
We also want to make sure
that we select the right file

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.160
format, CSV.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.740
We'll give our table a name,
names_2014, and toggle Edit

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.260
As Text to define a schema--

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:49.480
name, string, gender,
string, and count, integer.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.490
Click Create Table.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.710
Now that we've loaded
data into a table,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.210
we should be able to query it.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.140
Click Compose New Query.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.510
Let's find out the top five
male names in our table.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.820
Click Run, and we'll
get the results here

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.970
below the query window.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:12.140
And voila, that's a quick
start on BigQuery's web UI.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

